WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.455
Evaluation means measuring how an assistant performs on a collection of examples.

00:00:06.455 --> 00:00:15.458
One good conversation can be encouraging, but it cannot tell you whether a change helped other customers asking different questions.

00:00:15.458 --> 00:00:21.113
For each case, write the expected action and the important facts in the answer.

00:00:21.113 --> 00:00:25.725
Two polite replies can use different words and both be correct.

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:30.013
A polite reply that invents a delivery date should fail.

00:00:30.013 --> 00:00:35.509
To compare two versions, keep the cases and the rules for judging them the same.

00:00:35.509 --> 00:00:41.771
Then look at the particular cases that changed, as well as the total number that passed.

00:00:41.771 --> 00:00:45.415
Orbit prepares sixty customer requests for Scout.

00:00:45.415 --> 00:00:51.776
An order question should use the correct lookup and report the status found in the test record.

00:00:51.776 --> 00:00:55.204
A return request has different expectations.

00:00:55.204 --> 00:01:04.515
Run the first version on all sixty, starting each customer separately, and keep a record of which individual cases pass.

00:01:04.515 --> 00:01:06.569
Here is one test case.

00:01:06.569 --> 00:01:10.978
The question is real: Ravi asks whether his order has shipped.

00:01:10.978 --> 00:01:14.716
The case says which tool Scout must call: get order.

00:01:14.716 --> 00:01:19.989
It lists a fact the reply must contain: his order is still processing.

00:01:19.989 --> 00:01:27.311
And it lists something the reply must never contain: a tracking number, because Ravi doesn't have one yet.

00:01:27.311 --> 00:01:34.138
Good cases come from real tickets: the common ones, the awkward ones and the risky ones.

00:01:34.138 --> 00:01:36.842
The first run passes thirty-eight.

00:01:36.842 --> 00:01:44.442
Fifteen failures share a cause: customers supplied an email and the order lookup did not handle it correctly.

00:01:44.442 --> 00:01:47.931
After the change, fifty-three cases pass.

00:01:47.931 --> 00:01:56.390
Compare the lists of passing cases, not just the totals, to confirm which customers the change actually helped.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:00.074
Here is Scout's score history, one line per change.

00:02:00.074 --> 00:02:05.718
The first full run scored 38, and 15 of the failures shared one cause.

00:02:05.718 --> 00:02:12.120
Letting get order take an email lifted it to 53, and no case that passed before broke.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:19.332
Then a shorter system prompt dropped it to 50, breaking three return cases, so Priya undid it.

00:02:19.332 --> 00:02:23.817
A clearer rule about refund replies took it to 58.

00:02:23.817 --> 00:02:28.608
Without the history, that drop to 50 would have slipped through to customers.

00:02:28.608 --> 00:02:36.032
Scores can wobble a little between runs, so always look at which cases moved, not just the total.

00:02:36.032 --> 00:02:38.990
Picture sixty labelled envelopes.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:43.438
Each contains a question and the facts a passing answer needs.

00:02:43.438 --> 00:02:47.527
Using the same envelopes makes the two scores comparable.

00:02:47.527 --> 00:02:53.492
Swapping hard questions for easy ones would change the test as well as the model.

00:02:53.492 --> 00:02:56.602
Open the envelopes whose results changed.

00:02:56.602 --> 00:03:01.328
A drop from fifty-three passes to fifty tells you the net change.

00:03:01.328 --> 00:03:09.960
The count alone cannot tell you whether three old successes broke or several gains and losses cancelled each other out.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.368
Lay the affected return questions beside the prompt edit.

00:03:14.368 --> 00:03:19.426
Did a shorter instruction remove the rule those answers depended on?

00:03:19.426 --> 00:03:23.034
Rerun the original wording on the same cases.

00:03:23.034 --> 00:03:27.347
You now have evidence for deciding whether to keep the edit.

00:03:27.347 --> 00:03:30.936
Next the team shortens the assistant’s instructions.

00:03:30.936 --> 00:03:33.169
The score drops to fifty.

00:03:33.169 --> 00:03:36.960
Three return cases that used to pass now fail.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:41.880
Open those three and compare their replies with the return policy.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.979
Their common mistake guides the next edit.

00:03:44.979 --> 00:03:51.752
That is why keeping individual results is more useful than keeping only a percentage.
